# =============================================================================
# E2I Causal Analytics — Environment Variables
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env and fill in real values:
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Required vars are marked [REQUIRED]. Only four of them make compose refuse to
# START: REDIS_PASSWORD, FALKORDB_PASSWORD, SUPABASE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD (base) and
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD (interpolated even with the monitoring profile off).
# The rest fail at runtime instead. Check a candidate file without starting
# anything:  docker compose --env-file .env -f docker/docker-compose.yml config -q
#
# Symlink: docker/.env -> ../.env (so compose resolves ${VAR} references)
#
# NOT EVERYTHING HERE REACHES THE CONTAINERS. Compose forwards a WHITELIST
# (x-common-env in docker/docker-compose.yml), not this file. Vars marked
# "host-side only" below are read by host-run scripts and are inert in Docker.
# See section 12 at the end.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. CORE INFRASTRUCTURE — Memory Systems [REQUIRED]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_PASSWORD=changeme                   # [REQUIRED] Redis auth (working memory + Celery broker)
FALKORDB_PASSWORD=changeme                # [REQUIRED] FalkorDB auth (semantic memory graph)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. SUPABASE — Database + Auth [REQUIRED]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPABASE_URL=http://localhost:54321       # [REQUIRED] Supabase project URL
SUPABASE_KEY=your-anon-key-here           # [REQUIRED] Supabase anon/public key
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key-here      # Alias (some code reads this; set same as SUPABASE_KEY)
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=your-service-key     # [REQUIRED] Supabase service role key (admin)
# Password of the self-hosted supabase-db container — it mirrors POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# in /opt/supabase/docker/.env. Compose derives the container-internal
# SUPABASE_DB_URL from it (docker/docker-compose.yml x-common-env) and REFUSES to
# start without it: `docker compose --env-file .env -f docker/docker-compose.yml
# config` fails with "required variable SUPABASE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD is missing a
# value". Required since cd4317fa5 (2026-03-26).
SUPABASE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme       # [REQUIRED] self-hosted supabase-db password
# Optional and currently unused: src/api/dependencies/auth.py verifies tokens via
# the Supabase client's get_user(), not this secret, and compose does not forward
# it. The real "auth disabled" condition is a missing SUPABASE_URL/ANON_KEY.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=your-jwt-secret     # host-side only; optional
#
# HOST-SIDE ONLY. Compose derives its own in-network SUPABASE_DB_URL from
# SUPABASE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD above and ignores this value; it is here for
# host-run tools (scripts/run_migrations.sh psql mode, ad-hoc psql).
# This stack publishes supabase-db's 5432 on host port **5433** — host 5432 is the
# Supavisor pooler, which only accepts the `postgres.<project-ref>` user format.
# Verify:  docker port supabase-db
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@localhost:5433/postgres  # host-side tools only

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. LLM PROVIDERS [REQUIRED] — see docs/LLM_CONFIGURATION.md
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The default provider is OPENAI (src/utils/llm_factory.py): the default
# chat/reasoning path runs gpt-5.6-terra and the fast tier gpt-5.6-luna, so
# OPENAI_API_KEY is the key the stack requires out of the box. Anthropic is
# the alternative provider (set LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic).
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...                     # [REQUIRED] OpenAI API key (default provider)
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...            # Optional: Anthropic (alternative provider)
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai                     # openai (default) or anthropic
# LLM_MODEL=gpt-5.6-terra                 # Optional: pin the OpenAI standard/reasoning model
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5         # host-side only — deliberately NOT forwarded by compose
#                                         # (`grep -c 'ANTHROPIC_MODEL:' docker/docker-compose.yml` -> 0).
#                                         # Inside Docker the in-code default governs.
# DSPY_LM_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.6-terra      # Optional: explicit DSPy/litellm model (provider-prefixed)
#
# AS DEPLOYED on the droplet the two lanes are split on purpose (ADR-010):
#   LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic  and  DSPY_LM_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.6-terra
# so production needs BOTH keys. "LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic, therefore no OpenAI key"
# is wrong twice over — the DSPy pin and the RAG embeddings both need OpenAI.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. OPIK — LLM Observability [REQUIRED only for the Opik overlay]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: the Opik overlay is intentionally STOPPED in production (2026-05-29).
# These vars matter only if you run docker/docker-compose.opik.yml yourself.
# Production LLM observability is the llm_usage_events table + the /admin
# Observability tab (see docs/LLM_CONFIGURATION.md §4).
#
# WARNING: Opik credentials bake into Docker volumes at first startup.
# If you change passwords after volumes exist, wipe volumes first:
#   docker volume rm $(docker volume ls -q | grep opik)
#
OPIK_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=changeme         # [REQUIRED] MySQL root password
OPIK_MYSQL_PASSWORD=changeme              # [REQUIRED] MySQL 'opik' user password
OPIK_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=changeme         # [REQUIRED] ClickHouse 'opik' user password
OPIK_REDIS_PASSWORD=changeme              # [REQUIRED] Opik Redis password
OPIK_MINIO_ROOT_USER=opik-admin           # [REQUIRED] MinIO root username
OPIK_MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=changeme123      # [REQUIRED] MinIO root password (>= 8 chars!)
# OPIK_VERSION=1.10.8                     # Opik image tag (pinned in compose)
#
# OPIK_ENABLED is an opt-OUT switch: the in-code default is ON and the compose
# default is "true", so production must set it FALSE explicitly. Without that the
# health-score tracer and opik_connector build clients and leave forever-retrying
# uploader threads pointed at the intentionally-stopped opik-backend on every
# health check. Keep this line uncommented on the droplet.
OPIK_ENABLED=false                        # opt-OUT switch — production keeps this false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4b. ORCHESTRATOR ROUTING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORCHESTRATOR_CLASSIFIER_MODE=shadow     # 4-stage query classifier: off | shadow (default; classify+log, legacy routing) | active (classifier routes when confident)
# ROUTING_LABEL_MIN_NEW_ROWS=10           # Routing labeler (#1341): skip the nightly cycle below this many unlabeled classification_logs rows
# ROUTING_LABEL_JUDGE_CAP=50              # Routing labeler: max LLM-judge calls per run (token spend bound)
# ROUTING_LABEL_JUDGE_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001  # Routing labeler: judge model (needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; fail-open)
# ROUTING_LABEL_LOOKBACK_DAYS=30          # Routing labeler: how far back to look for unlabeled rows

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. OBSERVABILITY — Grafana [REQUIRED only with the monitoring profile]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Since #1806 prometheus, alertmanager, node-exporter, postgres-exporter, loki,
# promtail and grafana sit behind the `monitoring` compose profile: a plain
# `up -d` does not start them and no deploy step does either. Start them with
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=monitoring docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d
# The value must still be SET even with the profile off — compose interpolates
# every service definition before it filters by profile, so an unset
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD fails `config`/`up` regardless.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme           # [REQUIRED only with the monitoring profile — but must be set either way]
# SENTRY_DSN=                             # Optional: Sentry error tracking DSN

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. DEV TOOLS — Flower & Redis Commander [REQUIRED only with --profile dev-tools]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both services live in docker-compose.dev.yml behind the `dev-tools` profile and
# bind to 127.0.0.1 only. They never run in production.
FLOWER_USER=admin                         # [REQUIRED only with --profile dev-tools] Flower web UI username
FLOWER_PASSWORD=changeme                  # [REQUIRED only with --profile dev-tools] Flower web UI password

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. API CONFIGURATION
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENVIRONMENT and LOG_LEVEL are HARDCODED to production/INFO inside api, workers
# and scheduler by x-common-env — the values here feed only host-side runs and the
# compose `config-check` service.
ENVIRONMENT=development                   # host-side only: development | production | test
LOG_LEVEL=INFO                            # host-side only: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3002      # NO READER — nothing in src/ reads this var
# ENABLE_HSTS=false                       # host-side only (src/api/middleware/security_middleware.py); default true
# DISABLE_RATE_LIMITING=false             # host-side only; ignored when ENVIRONMENT=production

# Admin login used by golden-set replay + live e2e validation
# (scripts/replay_golden_set.py, scripts/sync_goldstd_serving.py,
#  frontend/e2e/live-goldstd-validation.spec.ts)
# E2I_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# E2I_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. GITHUB / DEPLOYMENT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
GITHUB_PAT=ghp_...                        # GitHub PAT (for git operations)
# DEPLOY_HOST=                            # SSH host (CI/CD only)
# DEPLOY_USER=                            # SSH user (CI/CD only)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. FRONTEND (Vite — set in docker-compose.dev.yml, override here if needed)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_API_URL=/api
# VITE_WS_URL=wss://eznomics.site
# VITE_COPILOT_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. ML / TRAINING FLAGS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Forwarded by compose since #1931, so the rollback below works in Docker too —
# it previously did not, and changed nothing silently. Note the compose entry is
# `${ADAPTIVE_CRITERIA:-true}`, mirroring the in-code default on purpose: the
# reader is `os.getenv("ADAPTIVE_CRITERIA", "true")`, so an empty compose default
# would put "" in the containers and turn the engine OFF platform-wide.
# Adaptive success criteria — PRODUCTION DEFAULT (true, flipped 2026-06-02).
# The v3 engine emits thresholds keyed on dataset characteristics
# (n_samples, prevalence, baseline_auc, feature_count, regime) and adopts the
# v3 contract: DROPS minimum_precision + minimum_f1 (Van Calster 2025), ADDS
# net-benefit + MCC + calibration slope/intercept gates. Set
# ADAPTIVE_CRITERIA=false to OPT OUT and reproduce the fixed Apr-26 baseline
# (minimum_auc/precision/recall/f1) exactly — that is also the ROLLBACK switch.
# See .claude/plans/adaptive_success_criteria/01-design.md for the formula.
ADAPTIVE_CRITERIA=true

# Expert-review enforcement on the causal_impact agent path (#1971). Forwarded
# by compose as `${CAUSAL_IMPACT_REQUIRE_DAG_APPROVAL:-}`; the reader treats ""
# like unset. Default OFF = post-hoc sign-off: a REVIEW-band (borderline)
# estimate queues its DAG structure for expert review, carries the gate
# decision (refutation.expert_review_decision) and a caveat, and the run
# continues. Set true to make a REVIEW-band run whose structure holds no active
# approval HALT (status failed, current_phase awaiting_expert_review, the review
# id and POST /expert-reviews/{id}/resolve in the message) until a reviewer
# resolves it. Independent of this switch, a structure a reviewer REJECTED
# halts on every band and is never promoted to validated. PROCEED-band
# estimates never need approval. Before turning it on, note the queue:
# 38 pending / 0 approved on 2026-09-08 -- every borderline analysis would wait
# on a human.
# CAUSAL_IMPACT_REQUIRE_DAG_APPROVAL=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. LAYER-4 ADAPTIVE-VALIDITY AUDIT EVALUATOR
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADAPTIVE_VALIDITY_EVALUATOR_ENABLED and _MODEL are forwarded by compose since
# #1931 (both default empty there, which their reader treats as unset), so
# setting either here now reaches the containers. The artifacts dir is set by
# compose to /app/data/audit_artifacts.
# Plan: .claude/plans/layer4_evaluator_audit_signal.md (producer, shipped
# 2026-05-15) + .claude/plans/layer4_evaluator_audit_consumer.md
# (persistence + curation CLI).
#
# Set to 1 to enable the Haiku-based audit-only evaluator that judges the
# worker's causal-role classifications. Default OFF.
# ADAPTIVE_VALIDITY_EVALUATOR_ENABLED=0

# Evaluator model identifier. Default is Anthropic Haiku 4.5.
# ADAPTIVE_VALIDITY_EVALUATOR_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# Where the adaptive-validity audit sidecars are written. When unset, the
# producer silently no-ops. In docker-compose this defaults to
# /app/data/audit_artifacts (mounted into the audit_artifacts named volume
# on every service that mounts agent_outputs). For local dev outside
# Docker, point at a directory the host filesystem owns.
# ADAPTIVE_VALIDITY_ARTIFACTS_DIR=./data/audit_artifacts

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. RUNTIME KNOBS FORWARDED BY COMPOSE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose forwards NO .env file wholesale. The x-common-env anchor in
# docker/docker-compose.yml is a WHITELIST: a variable set here reaches api,
# worker_* and scheduler only if that anchor names it. Anything else is a silent
# no-op in the containers — the in-code default governs and nothing warns you.
# Derive the current list rather than trusting this copy of it:
#
#   sed -n '/^x-common-env:/,/^x-common-worker:/p' docker/docker-compose.yml \
#     | grep -o '\${[A-Z_0-9]*' | tr -d '${' | sort -u
#
# Everything below is OPTIONAL and shown at its compose default — uncomment only
# to override. Full table with rationale: DEPLOYMENT.md § Environment Variables.

# --- Serving / boot ---------------------------------------------------------
# GUNICORN_PRELOAD=true                   # gunicorn preload (#1560 stream-tear fix); api only. Kill switch: false
# E2I_REQUIRE_FULL_AGENT_REGISTRY=false   # true => a partial agent registry raises PartialAgentRegistryError (#1448).
#                                         # One-boot post-deploy verification, not a steady-state setting
# CHATBOT_STARTUP_WARM_ENABLED=true       # per-worker warm of DSPy LM config, retrieval clients, registry, classifier (#1454)
# CHATBOT_STARTUP_WARM_LLM_ENABLED=true   # the warm's 2 synthetic-LLM legs; ~2 small completions per worker per boot (#1475)

# --- retrieve_rag chain ------------------------------------------------------
# CHATBOT_RAG_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=20            # fail-open ceiling per chain LLM call (rewrite/score/hop-decider) (#1484)
# CHATBOT_RAG_DRY_HOP_LIMIT=2             # consecutive zero-new-keep hops before the loop stops; 0 = run to max (#1484)
# CHATBOT_RAG_REWRITE_COT=                # empty = Predict-only rewriter; "true" restores ChainOfThought (#1518)
# CHATBOT_RAG_SKIP_EMPTY_DECIDER=         # empty/true = skip the decider call when hop-1 kept nothing; "false" restores it (#1518)

# --- Nightly DSPy/GEPA legs (spend real judge budget — operator cost decision) -
# DSPY_RAG_RECORDS_PATH=                  # RAG-prompt GEPA records file. RESOLVED INSIDE THE CONTAINER: must be a path under a
#                                         # named volume the service mounts rw (e.g. /app/optimized_modules). A host path skips
#                                         # forever while looking configured (#1486)
# DSPY_RAG_MAX_METRIC_CALLS=              # judge-call budget; empty on purpose — the in-code default (40) is the SSOT (#1486)
# DSPY_RAG_DB_FEEDSTOCK_ENABLED=          # live-traffic feedstock; the only way the cycle runs unattended. Fail-closed parse (#1489)
# DSPY_RAG_DB_LOOKBACK_DAYS=              # read window in days (in-code default 30) (#1489)
# CHATBOT_OPT_DRAIN_ENABLED=              # chatbot optimization queue drainer gate; unset = drain skipped (#1515)
# CHATBOT_OPT_DRAIN_MAX_PER_CYCLE=        # GEPA executions per cycle (in-code default 1) (#1515)
# CHATBOT_OPT_STALE_HOURS=                # in-code default 168 (#1515)
# CHATBOT_OPT_ZOMBIE_HOURS=               # in-code default 12 (#1515)
# CHATBOT_OPT_MIN_SIGNALS=                # producer minimum (in-code default 50) (#1515)

# --- Synthetic-data visibility ----------------------------------------------
# E2I_KPI_INCLUDE_SYNTHETIC=0             # KPI reads swap to the *_include_synthetic twins; the FE badges them as synthetic
# E2I_INCLUDE_SYNTHETIC=0                 # deployment-wide: every read-path chokepoint includes synthetic. Reversible

# --- Optional external biomedical API credentials (soft-degrade, never fatal) -
# NCBI_API_KEY=                           # absent: PubMed E-utilities on the anonymous ~3 req/s tier. Measured over 8 rapid
#                                         # esearch calls: 5/8 throttled (HTTP 429) without the key, 0/8 with it
# UMLS_UTS_API_KEY=                       # absent: umls_uts raises UMLSAuthError, CitationResolver degrades to umls=None
#                                         # (no synonym expansion — real citations can score as unverified)
# OPENFDA_API_KEY=                        # absent: unauthenticated client, no rate-limit headers. With the key: x-ratelimit-limit 240

# --- API compute pools + run budgets (forwarded since #1931) -----------------
# All empty by default in compose, which each reader treats as unset, so the
# in-code default governs until you set one. Sized against the container cgroup.
# AGENT_COMPUTE_EXECUTOR_WORKERS=       # agent-graph pool size (src/api/dependencies/compute.py)
# HEAVY_COMPUTE_MAX_CONCURRENCY=        # heavy-op admission budget, sized under the 5G cgroup
# HEAVY_COMPUTE_EXECUTOR_WORKERS=       # heavy executor pool; never outpaces the budget above
# SEGMENT_ANALYSIS_BUDGET_SECONDS=      # one segment graph fit (in-code default 900.0)
# HEAVY_OFFLOAD_ENABLED=                # P2 offload to worker_heavy. DARK by default; also
#                                       # needs worker_heavy scaled up (it ships at replicas: 0)

# --- Host-side only: deliberately NOT forwarded into the containers ----------
# These are absent from x-common-env on purpose, so setting them here affects
# host-run scripts only and the in-code default governs in Docker:
#   ANTHROPIC_MODEL   — the host pin is an id meant for interactive use, not the
#                       platform lanes. Container code must not read it either:
#                       there it can only return its own fallback (#1933).
#   CORS_ORIGINS      — no reader in src/ at all.
#   SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET — unused by the get_user() auth path.
# Verify any var's status with: grep -c '<VAR>:' docker/docker-compose.yml
